1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,275
What Makes Popcorn Pop?

2
00:00:02,225 --> 00:00:09,637
Popcorn is a distinct variety of maize (Zea mays everta) with a hard, non-porous outer hull called the pericarp.

3
00:00:09,637 --> 00:00:15,200
Unlike other corn varieties, its pericarp is thick enough to act as a pressure vessel.

4
00:00:15,200 --> 00:00:24,300
Inside each kernel is a small reservoir of water — approximately 14% of the kernel's mass — trapped within a matrix of starch granules.

5
00:00:24,300 --> 00:00:30,475
When heated to around 180 degrees Celsius, the water undergoes a phase change to steam.

6
00:00:30,475 --> 00:00:41,987
Because the pericarp is impermeable, the steam cannot escape, causing internal pressure to rise to roughly nine times atmospheric pressure, or approximately 1.3 megapascals.

7
00:00:41,987 --> 00:00:46,287
At this critical threshold the pericarp ruptures catastrophically.

8
00:00:46,287 --> 00:00:54,750
The sudden pressure drop causes the superheated starch to vaporize almost instantly, expanding up to 40 times the original kernel volume.

9
00:00:54,750 --> 00:01:00,625
The white, fluffy result is a solid foam—a network of cooled, gelatinized starch.

10
00:01:00,625 --> 00:01:09,862
High-speed cinematography has revealed that the kernel lifts off the surface by roughly one centimeter during the explosion, propelled by the escaping steam jet.

11
00:01:09,862 --> 00:01:16,437
The optimal popping temperature ranges from 175 to 185 degrees Celsius.

12
00:01:16,437 --> 00:01:28,037
Kernels with moisture below 12% produce insufficient pressure and result in unpopped kernels, while moisture above 16% causes premature shell failure and dense, chewy popcorn.

13
00:01:28,037 --> 00:01:38,075
Popcorn has been cultivated in the Americas for over 5,000 years; cobs dating to 3600 BCE were discovered in bat caves in New Mexico.

